xposure to things like sun and  pollution you'd want to keep the people  in specialized accommodation so they  couldn't like sneak out and get secret  midnight snacks you'd also want to run  the study for a good amount of time  because hormones and stress you want  that to be enough cycles that you could  get a good idea of whether it's the  chocolate or the stress or the hormones  causing any acne so probably at least  six months  anyway it turns out you're not allowed  to jail people for six months for  science even if you're paying them and  it turns out you have to pay them a lot  to convince people to let you control  every aspect of their lives  so this perfect setup never happens  nutrition studies have to make a lot of  compromises so it's a lot harder to be  confident in the results and because the  studies aren't really controlling all of  the variables a lot of other things can  affect the results so unsurprisingly a  lot of these studies have contradictory  results  so keeping in mind what our perfect  setup looks like let's have a look at  the two types of studies on food and  acne that we actually get the first type  are interventional studies where they  change people's diets and see what  happens  this sounds a bit like our ideal  situation except these studies usually  have less than 100 people often a lot  less we're talking an average of 30  something people in the trial that means  10 to 20 people in each group for  example 17 people getting chocolate and  17 not that is not a lot of people it's  really difficult to be confident there  wasn't some sort of fluke involved it's  quite possible that two people in the  chocolate group just happen to have  honking big pimples that week for some  other reason like it's exam week or it's  a particular point in their menstrual  cycle another issue with these studies  is that they usually don't tightly  control what the participants eat for  the chocolate studies usually they give  people a specific type and amount of  chocolate to eat but they don't tell  them what else they should be eating so  maybe having the chocolate meant they  didn't feel like eating as much fruit or  ice cream and it's actually the lack of  fruit or ice cream that's causing more  pimples a lot of these studies are also  on very specific groups of people for  example there are a lot of studies where  all of the participants are male 20  something college students in the us  most studies purposely use men because  these studies tend not to go for very  long because it's a lot cheaper that way  usually they're four weeks or less and  with women there's the menstrual cycle  and this has a massive effect on acne  the college students part is because  it's very easy to bribe college students  into doing a study so this very limited  group of people studied means that it's  difficult to see whether the results  would also apply to a woman in her  mid-30s like me or a teenage girl or a  male college student in japan we know  that genetics can have a really big role  in how people process food for example  lactose intolerance varies across  ethnicity and by country so generalizing  these studies which happens all of the  time on social media is a big problem  you'll see the results of these studies  turn into part of a cute infographic or  three seconds in a tik-tok video but  it's missing all of this extra context  three out of the four small studies on  chocolate published in the last 11 years  were done on just male college students  and there just aren't a lot of  interventional studies done on food and  acne a 2021 review looked at all of the  studies in the last 11 years and they  found 11 interventional studies that's  11 studies not just on chocolate and  acne but 11 interventional studies on  any dietary change in acne since 2009.  like i said good dietary studies are  expensive and complicated to run even  though these are still very far from  that ideal situation and even with all  of these compromises they're still rare  there is another type of study as well  that adds to what we know about food and  acne observational studies these look at  what people are already doing and try to  find patterns so for example do people  who eat more of a certain type of food  tend to have more acne there are more of  these studies because they're less of a  pain to run and they tend to have more  participants and more diverse  participants but they have a lot of  other issues because the people are just  going about their everyday lives as the  scientists record what they eat and how  their skin's going that means that  firstly there is a massive amount of  variation in what people are eating and  doing during the study there are a lot  of little things that could be impacting  the results that won't be recorded in  the study secondly there's a big issue  with recording what people ate and what  happened to their skin  in observational studies participants  usually have to tell the scientists what  they ate now if you know you have to  record your food it might change what  you eat and you might self-edit your  food diary a bit and if you've ever had  to record your food intake for whatever  reason you'll know how hard it is to  record every single thing you eat and if  you go to a restaurant or get takeaway  it is impossible to work out how much of  each ingredient you're eating i recently  had some random food allergies that i  haven't quite figured out yet and the  number of times i've told myself i'll  write it down later and then just  straight up forgot is ridiculous and a  lot of the time the studies don't get  people to record their food they get  them to fill out a survey afterwards the  problem here is that if it's already  hard to remember what you ate today it  is really really hard to remember what  you ate over the last two weeks  some of these food and acne studies got  people to remember what they ate years  ago the most highly cited study on dairy  and acne got their data by asking women  in their 30s and 40s what they ate when  they were teenagers and so all of these  issues make it harder to draw strong  conclusions about what food caused what  effect with these observational studies  let's take a look at a recent  observational study as an example  scientists got 24 and a half thousand  people in france to track what they ate  over three days at the start middle and  end of a year the people were also asked  whether or not they had acne and the  scientists found that there was a  statistically significant association  between current acne and eating fatty  and sugary foods sugary drinks and milk  so at face value it seems like this  study is saying that more sugar leads to  more acne but if we take a closer look  we have lots of people which is really  good the study used surveys for  recording food which has the usual  issues with imperfect recall and  self-editing there's also the fact that  most people don't eat the same thing  consistently over a week let alone a  year so these three day snapshots might  not reflect their usual diet and then  there's also that other data point the  acne out of the people who said they had  acne 44 diagnose themselves only 37 were  diagnosed by dermatologists and even  dermatologists get it wrong so it's very  possible that a lot of these people  didn't actually have acne it could be  something that looks similar like  folliculitis or rosacea on top of this  there are a lot of other factors that  could be messing up the results three  quarters of the people were female which  means there are lots of hormonal factors  that could skew the results for example  there's pcos which affects somewhere  around 10 of women of reproductive age  and this is strongly linked to acne  there's also hormonal contraceptives  which can increase or decrease acne and  roughly half of the women in france use  hormonal contraceptives eating more  sugary foods could also lead to people  eating less of other things like  vegetables and fruit and it might be the  fact that they're having less of these  things that could be leading to more  acne and not the sugary food itself  eating more sugar could also be a  reflection of something else that's the  actual root cause of the acne people who  eat less sugar might also be more health  conscious in the rest of their lives so  for example they might have a closer  relationship with their doctor they  might exercise more they might have a  less stressful lifestyle correlation  isn't necessarily causation the  association that the scientists found  between acne and these fatty and sugary  foods sugary drinks and milk was also  quite weak which means it's difficult to  say whether there would still be this  association if we took out some of these  extra factors  so with all of this background you can  hopefully appreciate how hard it is to  confidently say much about links between  food and acne and that's why dermatology  organizations are so careful in their  recommendations about changing your diet  to manage your acne the strongest links  that we have with this rather flawed  evidence are pretty weak associations  which means it's hard to say that eating  these foods actually cause the acne and  that stopping these foods will also stop  acne but these are the two things we  have the most evidence for firstly high  glycemic index and glycemic load diets  these have lots of refined sugar and  carbohydrates that absorb quickly into  your bloodstream and they seem to be the  strongest link there is between diet and  acne it's still a weak link out of the  three interventional studies on low gi  gl diets and acne two of them found a  relationship but one of them found no  significant difference in acne with high  and low gi  the second thing is dairy high intake of  dairy products seems to be associated  with acne this is a pretty confusing one  because dairy is a broad category and  different studies define it in different  ways it seems like low fat and skim milk  are more strongly linked to acne than  full fat dairy  yogurt and cheese don't seem to be  linked but whey protein does seem to be  linked with both high gi gl diets and  dairy the proposed ways that they could  be leading to acne are pretty similar  both of them can increase the levels of  igf-1 and insulin in blood higher igf-1  can increase androgen hormones  specifically the male hormones  testosterone and dht these can increase  the production of sebum which is the  natural oil in your skin that can clog  pores and increased sebum is one of the  contributors to acne higher igf-1 can  also lead to overgrowth of keratinocyte  cells in your skin which means you have  more stuff in your skin that can lead to  clogged pores both of these diets could  also lead to inflammation which is  another one of the contributors to acne  unfortunately all of this extra context  doesn't really make for a great viral  tic toc video  so how can we use this information the  science on the link between food and  acne is really not that solid which  isn't that different from a lot of  things in skincare there are a lot of  gray areas but the difference between  skin care and food is that adding or  removing a skincare product generally  isn't a big deal changing your diet is a  much bigger deal especially when it  comes to the two types of food we have  the most evidence for dietary changes  can affect a lot more than just your  skin  high gi foods and dairy are lots of  common foods so being very black and  white and restricting yourself to a  fraction of the foods you used to eat  can easily lead to nutritional  deficiencies and a wildly unbalanced  diet  and more importantly it can have a  massive impact on your mental health as  well in particular with disordered  eating for example you've probably heard  of orthorexia which is essentially an  unhealthy obsession with eating the  correct foods one of the first published  case studies on orthorexia was a woman  whose disordered eating started when she  started cutting out fat from her diet to  control her acne and there are lots of  examples in the literature and around  the internet where people who changed  their diets to try to kill their acne  ended up triggering an eating disorder  or making one worse it's not uncommon on  skincare forums to see people talking  about what foods they can't eat or how  hard it is to avoid these foods how  they're avoiding going out with their  friends to control their temptation how  much guilt they're feeling when they eat  one piece of the so-called wrong food so  with all of this in mind all the issues  with the evidence and the potential  dangers you want to be very cautious  when messing around with your diet to  try to clear your skin  the evidence is not robust enough at  this moment in time to recommend dietary  changes alone for the management of acne  the official recommendation is if you  notice a food that might be making your  acne worse try not eating it for a short  time and see what happens but if you do  want to change your diet to see if it  impacts your skin here are three tips  from dr anjali mato tip number one be  careful with bad swaps that might make  things worse there are a small select  group of people that may be sensitive to  dairy so if you do want to cut dairy out  of your diet and you want to switch to a  plant-based alternative  maybe rather than jumping from dairy to  oat milk which is a common swap that i  see people making  think about unsweetened soy or almond  milk instead  now the reason for that is oat milk has  actually got a very high glycemic index  tip number two if you drink whey protein  shakes see if you can use a different  protein source i think that's one  situation where people may benefit again  from switching to a plant-based protein  instead  particularly because the amount of cow's  milk protein that you effectively get in  a whey protein supplement is actually  quite significantly high we're not  talking about a little splash in your  coffee or a little bit in your porridge  and the third tip  think about maybe bringing in an expert  if you are genuinely thinking of  removing things from your diet you  should really do it in conjunction with  a professional dietitian  if you start removing lots of things you  may end up with essentially a diet that  is missing a lot of important nutrients  that you might need for your overall  general health which is obviously a  situation that we want to avoid and be  really careful when you're taking and  spreading simplified diet device there  can be very real harms involved so i see  a lot of patients that will cut out  huge food groups and usually it is dairy  gluten and sugar and if you look hard  enough if you cut those three things out  according to the internet you can fix  every skin condition going and it gets  to the point where their skin's not  getting any better they're getting  really anxious about the foods that they  can and can't eat and what ends up then  happening is people find themselves in a  situation where their diet becomes more  and more restrictive  there is more anxiety around food and  almost the development of disordered  eating patterns because you become so  paranoid that anything that you might  eat might trigger your acne so if  changing your diet isn't a great way to  try to fix your acne what might be a  better idea i think if you are going to  try and manage your acne at home before  you seek professional help i think  actually looking at your skin care  routine is a far better starting point  than starting to cut things out of your  diet now if you have changed your  skincare up you've been doing it for a  good four to six weeks but you're still  finding that your acne is not getting  any better  or it's getting worse  or it's becoming more extensive so it's  not just affecting the face but it's  extending onto the neck the back the  chest the shoulders  if it's starting to scar or leave  permanent marking and about 20 of people  who have acne will get permanent  scarring  or if it's starting to affect your  mental health you know it's affecting  your day-to-day and your confidence  all of those then are signs that you  should then move on to professional help  you've done what you can at home and  you've given that a good bash  and unfortunately this makes for an even  worse tick tock video if you like this  video give it a thumbs up subscribe and  click the notification bell for more  videos like this probably with less  dancing thank you so much to dr anjali  mato for helping me out on this video  i've linked the full interview in the  description her insights are always  really level-headed her instagram is  absolutely fantastic as well definitely  one of my top recommendations for  dermatologists to follow

